{"id": "sevt_011uUxjsv2Vft9hgrCZpWUuB", "content": [{"text": "The root cause is the Stripe API, which started responding slowly at about 14:41. No deploy lines up with the spike.\n\n**Deploys**\n- The last checkout deploy was `0093e5e` (structured request logging) at 13:05. Checkout p99 stayed at about 75 ms for 96 minutes after it.\n- The only other deploy was auth `fed7560` at 10:12.\n\n**Latency and pool metrics**\n- Checkout p99 jumped from about 75 ms to a flat 5,030 ms at 14:41. That is the 5 s upstream timeout, not gradual degradation.\n- Checkout `db_pool_utilization` rose only after the spike, from 0.13 at 14:41 to a plateau of 0.72 by 14:46. That looks like requests holding connections while they wait on Stripe, not a database problem.\n- Cart, auth and inventory pool utilization stayed flat at 0.1\u20130.2 throughout.\n\n**Log confirmation**\n- The first slow Stripe call is at 14:41:10, with `upstream_call stripe-api latency_ms=4990` on checkout-3.\n- The first 5 s timeout error is at 14:42:24, followed by 502s with `UpstreamTimeout`.\n- All 164 `stripe-api` log lines are from checkout. Cart, auth and inventory don't call Stripe.\n- The only other warning in the window is a 243 ms GC pause on auth-1, which is too small to matter.\n\n**Recommended follow-ups**\n- Check Stripe's status page.\n- Consider a shorter timeout or circuit breaker on the Stripe call, since the full 5 s wait is what's holding checkout connections.\n\n> **Root cause:** `stripe-api` \u2014 Stripe calls from checkout began taking about 5 s from 14:41 and hit the 5000 ms timeout, which pushed checkout p99 to about 5 s and returned 502s. The checkout DB pool rise is a side effect.", "type": "text"}], "processed_at": "2026-10-14T05:53:02.369914Z", "type": "agent.message"}
